Z||000005||Fiero Fanatic||01-10-2001||01:31 AM||||Get a fuel pump pressure kit at your local auto parts store. Your fuel pump should build enough pressure after 2 seconds. It's possible your relay is stuck in the open position and stays on.||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||01-10-2001||12:44 PM||||on this same subject... ive heard that a Camaro V-6 pump is a bolt in replacement, but higher output, anyone know for sure?||||reg||1
Z||000007||william_thorniii||01-10-2001||12:49 PM||||roger,<P>As far as I know, ANY GM in tank pump will physically fit.  I do not know the answer to the flow rate question.  <P>Friends,<P>Could Mikes problem be some sort of leaking injector or similar that is making the system difficult to pressurize?<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||14
Z||000008||terryk||01-10-2001||01:07 PM||||The 3800 SC pump I installed for my swap wouldn't fit. I had to modify the float pivot point to get it into the tank. The pump housing is much larger.<P>Whatever you do, make sure you keep the existing mounts and cushions for the pump (as much as possible), otherwise you could make the pump whine in the passenger compartment unbearable. <P>Unless the engine is modified to consume more fuel, leave it alone.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Got2manyfieros||01-10-2001||01:42 PM||||OKay, so I'm having the same problem with my new fuel pump...does anyone know where the fuel pump relay is?   Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000010||LS1swap||01-10-2001||06:55 PM||||The relay is located on the fire wall to the right of the air filter. there are actuly two relays there the other is the AC. They are the same part so if you think your fuel pump relay is bad you can switch the wires and see . odds are they won't both be bad. Also if you don't have oil pressure after about 20 sec. the ECM will cut the power to the relay.||||reg||1
Z||000011||GT Bastard||01-10-2001||07:17 PM||||I've seen the same problem on two Fiero's.... It's the relay||||reg||1
Z||000012||mrfiero||01-10-2001||08:31 PM||||OK.....a couple of things.  I replaced the relay with another one......unless it was bad too, it didn't help. <P>I also found my fuel pressure gauge.  The pressure reads 45 psi and drops to 38 psi when idling.  I believe this is normal.....anyone?<P>What I gather from this info is my fuel injectors are OK (since the pressure remained constant).  That would mean that the fuel pump itself is bad.....right?<P>I also tried to get an emissions test today.  It failed..... high CO & HC.  The HC limit is 3.0, I had 14.6 and the CO limit is 20.0, I had 24.8.  The NOx limit is 6.0 and I passed with a 1.22.<P>I figure that the catalytic convertor is OK, otherwise the NOx would likely have failed too.....does that make sense?<P>Would a bad fuel pump cause these high readings or is that just wishful thinking?<P>Thanks.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||ray b||01-10-2001||10:12 PM|||| # sound rich lean would be high nox. stuck rich start injector? if stuck open could pump keep up with it? just a guess  ray||||reg||1
Z||000014||Fiero Fanatic||01-14-2001||09:29 AM||||Is your fuel pump running or not. How did your car run on the way to the emission place? If you set your timing to 8 degrees before TDC without plugging into the ECM, you should pass the emissions. your car will run like s**t, but a least you'll pass. After you pass and leave, pull over to the side of the road and then plug in the ECM and set the timing according to the book. But you didn't hear any of this from me.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.eccentrix.com/business/cityselect/84fiero.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Fiero Fanatic (edited 01-14-2001).]||||reg||1
